00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.790 align:start position:0%
 
no one person has the same goal or  aspiration and what you get when you  give people money and let them use it is  this real rich human experience where  everyone does something a bit different  hi I'm Anthony Fischer with reason TV  and I'm joined by Paul Niehaus the  president and co-founder of give  directly Paul thanks for joining us my  pleasure  what's give directly give directly is a  relatively new non-profit organization  with a really different concept of how  we can use money to help people overseas  who don't have much we take money from  donors and give it directly to extremely  poor people living in East Africa and  then they get to decide what to do with  it not us what was the impetus behind  starting this yeah you know we got going  when the co-founders and I were in grad  school doing graduate degrees in  economic development and we really  wanted to rethink this basic question of  how can we use money to help people who  don't have much of it and when you put  it that way it's obvious that one thing  you could do is give it to them and let  them use it but typically that's not  what we do right instead what we've  built is almost this benevolent  bureaucracy right of these large  international organizations that  fundamentally are there to make  decisions on behalf of the poor about  what they should get you know should  they get a cow should they get training  should they get a loan so the the money  transfers take place via cell phone what  percentage of the world's poorest  actually have cell phones  well first it's surprisingly high when  we show up in a community and these are  some of the poorest people in the world  so they're living on an average of  around 65 nominal cents a day right so  that means like your morning latte is  probably one week's budget for a typical  recipient many of these people will  still have at least access to a phone  around half of the people that we meet  but you don't actually even need one all  people literally need is a SIM card that  they can plug into someone else's phone  to receive transfers where does the  money come from originally us so when we  first got going it was literally just  friends and family you know we wanted to  be able to give our money this way  because we felt really compelled by the  evidence that it's a very effective way  of giving and just could not find  anybody that would let us give our own  money that way you know it's just very  counter to the way things are typically  done over time what's happened is since  we've launched it publicly the three  years ago is just really rapid growth to  around 20 million in a revenue right now  we're running and I'd say a lot of that  is coming from kind of people in our  generation people who are making money  working in sort of quantitative  industries like finance technology  people who really sort of believe in  quantitative approaches and care about  evidence but I think even an even more  basic level people who want something  that  more authentic and more honest than you  know the traditional way that we've  thought about giving can you estimate  how much more money you think gets  directly to the people through your app  than through traditional charities yeah  that's the question we all want to know  the answer to the reason it's so hard  frankly is because nonprofit accounting  is typically such a mush right so you  can go and get these IRS numbers like  percent overhead percent program  services the problem is they really  don't mean much right so even if you  take the percent going to program  services which what we typically think  about that basically just means how much  money is going to Africa and then all of  that could be going to salaries or  office space or it just doesn't measure  like value created for the recipients so  what we've done is we said something  very simple we're just gonna measure how  efficient we are by what fraction of  every dollar that you give us we put  into the hands of a recipient in Africa  and right now we're running at around 91  cents so I can't compare that but I  think it would be tough to beat the  critics might say that if you don't give  charitable money to a third party then  the recipients might spend it on things  like drugs or alcohol what in your  experience are is how's the money being  spent yeah this is really the core of  the story you know and the reason that  we got going so something important  happened in development around 2000  which is that we started to do  scientific experimental testing right  and it's kind of amazing because we've  been doing development for 50 years and  hadn't really done that very much and  what's great is that when you start  doing science when you sort of put two  things head-to-head and ask what's the  impact you know there are surprises  right so some things that you thought  were gonna work really well turn out not  to and some things that you thought  wouldn't work turn out to work really  well and I think that cash transfer is  just literally giving money to poor  people has turned out to be one of those  really big positive surprises over the  last decade to the point where I think  they're now sort of a stronger more  rigorous scientific evidence base in  favor of cash transfers than just about  any other tool that we have in the  development toolbox so in particular a  lot of those negative stereotypes that  we have people are just gonna get drunk  or they're gonna waste it on gambling  things like that not only has there been  no evidence to suggest that but there's  been if anything evidence I think of the  opposite what kind of data what kind of  evidence do you have what are they  spending the money on yeah so you know  what we see in our data which i think is  great is this real diversity in you know  in some studies we've seen impacts on  health reductions in HIV in others we  seeing these long-term increases in  earnings when you look at our data you  know you'll see there are some people  who buy cows it's about 30% of the  sample there are a lot of people who  invest in upgrading their housing buy  durable assets people's incomes go up by  around 30% for every every dollar that  they receive and then there are crazy  things you know there's one guy who  bought musical instruments and started a  band and tourists so I think you know  part of the story here is that on  average people are doing good things and  their lives are getting better but the  other part of the story is that you know  there really is no on average right  everybody is very different no one  person has the same goal or aspiration  and what you get when you give people  money and let them use it is this real  rich human experience where everyone  does something a bit different is any  part of give directly a reaction to say  bureaucratic waste among you know larger  charities yeah you know I think it's the  way I think about this is you know we do  have this very top-heavy bureaucratic  system there's a lot of overhead in it  and frankly nobody really knows how much  overhead right there are no data to tell  us so it's kind of a guess you know how  much is going to get through and what  it's going to get spent on I I'm  somewhat sympathetic I think that you  know if you looked at the world ten  years ago without the evidence we now  have and without the technology right  that lets us put money electronically  into someone's hands if they have a sim  card you might say you know maybe this  is the best approach the best we can do  but I think those two big trends are the  new evidence and the new technology  really make me step back in question you  know I think we can probably do  something much better how do you get the  word out to people in East Africa how  are they aware of give directly how are  they lining themselves up with the  communication to receive funds we go  door to door so we're primarily a field  organization and that's a bit different  from a lot of other startup nonprofits  recently that are more focused on the  fundraising the marketing side so you  know we control all of our own  operations that means that we have guys  who were going door-to-door in East  Africa they're signing people up for the  program they're capturing data digitally  which were then routing back into our  systems we're auditing we're looking for  fraud and then signing them up for these  mobile payment systems that let them get  money on their phone moving ahead how do  you plan on selling give directly to  America or the first world how are you  going to convince people that this is  the future giving people cash for them  to spend it on their on whatever they  see fit yeah we're you know I'm really  excited about this and I think I'd be  perfectly honest that over the first few  years we've really just focused on kind  of reaching out communicating to people  like us people  pretty quantitative if you like to lead  research papers who care about numbers  so it's been great to connect with that  community I think we have a wonderful  support base but um you know we also  think there's a much more human side to  this that we do want people to be able  to experience so longer-term you know  with all the data that we have in our in  our databases you know we have people's  photographs we have their stories we  know what their aspirations are their  goals their challenges and we know when  something goes wrong so we know if  someone has to pay a bribe or if there's  a prod their house burns down if there's  a bad outcome and we really want people  to get that unfiltered view right I  think that the way the poor are  presented to us today through nonprofit  marketing is this very stylized spun  version where we pick a few success  stories and they say that's the way it  is we'd like you to be able to see for  the specific people that I gave my money  to here's what actually happened and  there will be successes and failures and  things that you never would have  expected and I think it's an exciting  adventure has there been any pushback on  the ground in East Africa or from other  charitable organizations that feel like  your model doesn't work or that you're  chiseling a no matter territory you know  it's a funny thing you know because of  all this evidence cash transfers are now  widely accepted by governments by  institutions by the kind of eight  agencies of the world less so by the  typical American on the street it's  still a bit of a new idea so we've heard  criticism we've had people tell us you  guys must be smoking crack you know  anything you can imagine but I think  those are the good conversations to have  right it's great to get out and talk  about what are our preconceptions and  what are the data actually say and and I  relish it thanks so much for talking to  us Paul Niehaus the president of give  directly for Reason TV I'm Anthony  Fischer  you

